WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.360
Prepare, open well, trade every step, and listen.

2
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:08.850
This is the video for Module 2 of Negotiation Skills for Business and Life.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:16.200
Twenty minutes of preparation with pen and paper will usually earn you more than an hour of clever talking.

4
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:28.140
In this module you learn why the first number has so much power, how to give ground without giving away your profit, and how listening reveals what the other side really needs.

5
00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:37.990
Use one sheet for every important negotiation. Start with your goal in one sentence, why you want it, and what the other side might need.

6
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.910
Write your numbers. Your BATNA, walk-away point, target, and the opening offer you will make.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:58.790
Collect evidence. Send the same short WhatsApp message to three suppliers, and write the lowest, highest and typical price. Prices move quickly, so this week's evidence beats last year's memory.

8
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:11.110
List tradeables. These are things you can give or ask for besides price: quantity, payment timing, delivery, packaging. The best ones are cheap for you but valuable to them.

9
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.270
Finally, write your opening line and three questions, and say them aloud twice.

10
00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:26.730
Here is a worked example. Aisha's distributor says flour is going up from 68000 to 74000 naira a bag.

11
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.440
On 8 bags, that is 48000 more every week.

12
00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:43.930
Two other distributors quote lower, but charge for delivery. So her BATNA is about 72625 a bag. Anything above that, and she should switch.

13
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:54.820
She opens at 70000, with tradeables ready. She can order 16 bags every two weeks, pay on delivery, or collect from the depot herself.

14
00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:03.500
They settle at 71500 for fortnightly orders. That saves her 40000 over two weeks.

15
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:13.620
Psychologists call it anchoring. People are pulled toward the first number they hear. So when you have good information about market prices, go first.

16
00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:19.910
When you know very little, let them go first. Their number may be better than you expected.

17
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:28.070
Open above your target when selling, but close enough to explain. Say the number and its reason in the same breath.

18
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:38.660
If they open with an extreme number, do not move a little from it. Calmly say that is far from what the market shows, and name your own justified number.

19
00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:53.460
Grace's walk-away is 430000 naira and her target is 470000. The client offers 400000. Grace does not jump. She asks what the budget is made up of.

20
00:02:54.710 --> 00:03:00.280
She had opened at 520000, including servers, cooler hire and clean-up.

21
00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:07.710
Now she trades rather than gives. If you provide your own drinks, I can do 490000.

22
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:16.450
Then a smaller step. 475000 with small chops, if you pay a 60% deposit this week.

23
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:30.690
They agree at 470000. Her steps shrank, 30000, then 15000, then 5000, which told the client the limit was near. And every step was traded for something.

24
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:40.290
Use the if, then rule. If you can pay upfront, or take 20 pieces, then I can adjust the price. Every request has a cost.

25
00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:53.140
Avoid the common mistakes. Do not say my lowest is this. Do not split the difference when the middle is below your target. And never make two concessions in a row without getting anything back.

26
00:03:54.390 --> 00:04:06.570
Now listening. Ifeoma sells beaded necklaces at 12000 naira each at a craft fair in Abuja. A buyer wants three and says the price is too high. Ifeoma mirrors. Too high?

27
00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:18.230
The buyer saw some for 7000 online. Ifeoma labels her worry, then explains. Those are usually plastic. These are glass beads with a metal clasp.

28
00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:27.210
She asks the budget, 30000, and offers a trade: 32000 for all three, with matching earrings for the bride.

29
00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:33.890
The buyer pays by transfer, and Ifeoma checks the alert in her own bank app before handing over the goods.

30
00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:42.570
To remember. Prepare on one page with three current prices. Make concessions in shrinking steps, and trade every one.

31
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:50.210
Ask open questions that start with what or how. After you state your price, stop talking and count slowly to five.

32
00:04:51.460 --> 00:05:02.830
Now your turn. Complete a preparation sheet for a real negotiation, plan a concession ladder with shrinking steps, and practise listening in one small negotiation this week.

33
00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.120
Continue with the lessons below.
